    "47577": {
        "order_id": 30,
        "exam_type": "WAEC",
        "subject": "Chemistry",
        "topic": "Electrochemistry",
        "question_type": "Objective",
        "exam_year": "2004",
        "question_text": "<p>What is the standard electrode potential ($E\u00b0$) for the reduction of $Zn^{2+}$ to $Zn$ if the cell potential for $Zn|Zn^{2+}||Cu^{2+}|Cu$ is $+1.10 V$ and $E\u00b0$ for $Cu^{2+}/Cu$ is $+0.34 V$?</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>The standard cell potential ($E\u00b0_{cell}$) is given by $E\u00b0_{cell} = E\u00b0_{cathode} - E\u00b0_{anode}$. Here, $E\u00b0_{cell} = 1.10 V$ and $E\u00b0_{cathode} (Cu^{2+}/Cu) = +0.34 V$. Solving for $E\u00b0_{anode} (Zn^{2+}/Zn)$: $1.10 = 0.34 - E\u00b0_{anode}$, so $E\u00b0_{anode} = 0.34 - 1.10 = -0.76 V$.</p>",
        "reference_passage": null,
        "novel": null,
        "comments_count": 0,
        "options": [
            {
                "option_id": "94671",
                "tag": "a",
                "text": "$+0.76 V$",
                "is_correct": false
            },
